---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { RevenueChart } from '../../components/islands/RevenueChart'
import { OecdChart } from '../../components/islands/OecdChart'
import { debt, revenue, oecd, vintageOf } from '../../data'

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.slice(1, 9).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}

  <section id="where-money-comes-from">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 10</span></p>
    <h2>The mix changed. The total did not.</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Federal revenue was 17.9% of GDP in FY1995 and 17.2% in FY2025. Thirty years apart,
      essentially the same take. What moved is who pays it and through which tax.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Share of GDP, FY1995 to FY2025: individual income 7.81% to 8.75%, payroll 6.41% to
      5.76%, corporate 2.08% to 1.49%, customs 0.26% to 0.64%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Federal revenue by source held near 17 to 18 percent of GDP from FY1995 to FY2025 while the mix shifted toward the individual income tax and customs duties."
      title="Federal revenue by source, fiscal 1962 to fiscal 2025"
      source={revenue._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(revenue._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="nominal $ trillions, percent of GDP, or percent of total revenue"
    >
      <RevenueChart client:visible rows={revenue.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The individual income tax now raises more than half of all federal revenue, up from
      43.7% in 1995. The corporate share fell from 11.6% to 8.6%. Customs duties went from
      $19 billion to $195 billion, more than a tenfold nominal rise, and are the fastest
      growing revenue line in the federal government.
    </p>

    <p class="prose">
      The OECD figure below counts federal, state and local tax revenue together; the
      17.2%-of-GDP federal figure above does not include state and local collections. The
      two are not two readings of the same thing, and this section does not present them as
      a contradiction.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="The United States collected 25.6% of GDP in tax in 2024, 31st of 38 OECD members and below the 34.1% OECD average."
      title="Total tax revenue as a share of GDP, OECD comparison, 2024"
      source={oecd._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(oecd._meta)}
      xUnit="Percent of GDP"
      yUnit="Country or OECD average"
      note="Counts federal, state and local government together; not comparable to the federal-only revenue figure above. Shows ten selected members of 38; 2024 is preliminary."
    >
      <OecdChart client:visible data={oecd.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Counting federal, state and local together, the US collected 25.6% of GDP in tax in
      2024 against an OECD average of 34.1%, ranking 31st of 38 members. The US peaked at
      28.3% in 2000 and bottomed at 22.9% in 2009. It is also the only OECD member with no
      value-added tax, which accounts for most of the gap.
    </p>

    <p class="prose">
      Two dating exceptions to the FY1995/FY2025 convention used elsewhere in this route:
      the OECD figure above ends at 2024 preliminary data. Who pays the individual income
      tax by income group is a tax year 2023 IRS figure; it belongs to the households route,
      not this one.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.slice(10).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
